    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The arithmetic is short: ceiling area times coats, divided by the coverage on the can, plus a little extra. What makes people run out halfway through, or end up with three spare gallons, is using the label's best-case coverage on a surface that will drink more paint than that. Popcorn texture, new drywall and a big color change all push the real number up. Here is how to account for them.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="formula" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Basic Formula
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gallons needed equals the ceiling area in square feet, multiplied by the number of coats, divided by the paint's coverage in square feet per gallon. Round up, and add a margin for waste. That is the whole calculation, and for most smooth ceilings in good condition it gives a reliable number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage figures on paint cans are usually given as a range, commonly somewhere around 350 to 400 square feet per gallon for interior latex paint on a smooth, primed surface. Those are manufacturers' estimates under good conditions. Real coverage depends on the surface, the roller, how heavily you apply the paint and the product itself, so it is wise to use the lower end of the label range rather than the higher one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceilings are easier than walls because there is nothing to subtract. With walls you remove doors and windows; with a ceiling, the area is just length times width for a rectangular room. Odd shapes, vaulted ceilings and soffits take a little more work, which the next section covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Primer is calculated separately. If the ceiling needs a full coat of primer, for new drywall or stain blocking, work out the primer the same way using its own coverage figure, which is often similar to or slightly lower than paint. If you only need to spot-prime patches, a quart is usually enough for a typical room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FORMULA</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Area x coats / coverage, then round up.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use the low end of the label's coverage range, round up to whole gallons and add roughly 10 percent for waste and touch-ups. Then adjust for texture and porosity as described below.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="measure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Measuring the Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a rectangular room, measure the floor. The ceiling area is the same as the floor area, so length times width in feet gives square feet. A 12 by 14 foot bedroom has a 168 square foot ceiling. Measuring at floor level is easier and safer than measuring overhead, and closets are easy to add on separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            For L-shaped or irregular rooms, split the floor into rectangles, measure each one and add them together. Include closets, alcoves and bay windows if you are painting their ceilings too. It is easy to forget a walk-in closet, and a 6 by 8 foot closet adds 48 square feet, which is more than a tenth of a gallon per coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vaulted and sloped ceilings have more area than the floor below them. For a simple gable ceiling, measure the slope length from the wall to the peak and multiply by the room length, then double it for both sides. If measuring the slope is impractical, a rough adjustment is to add 20 to 40 percent to the floor area, depending on how steep the pitch is, but measuring is more reliable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tray ceilings, coffers and soffits add vertical surfaces. Measure the height of each vertical band and multiply by its length, then add those areas to the flat ceiling area. If these features will be a different color from the ceiling, calculate them separately so you buy the right amount of each.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ceiling shape</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How to measure</th>
