                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">L-shaped</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Split into rectangles, add them</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple vaulted (gable)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slope length x room length x 2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tray or coffered</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat area + height x length of each band</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Closets and alcoves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Measure and add separately</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Coverage per Gallon: Using the Label Sensibly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read the coverage on the specific product you are buying. It is usually printed on the can and on the manufacturer's product data sheet, often as a range such as 350 to 400 square feet per gallon. Ceiling paints are generally in the same ballpark as wall paints, though thick, high-solids ceiling paints sometimes quote slightly lower spread rates because they go on as a heavier film.
          </p>

          <p>
            The label number assumes a smooth, sound, primed surface and application at the recommended film thickness. Rolling more heavily than that, which many people do on ceilings to get better hide in one pass, uses paint faster. Rolling too thinly stretches coverage but often means an extra coat, so it rarely saves paint in the end.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical approach is to use 350 square feet per gallon for a smooth ceiling in good condition if the label gives a range starting there, and lower than that for anything rougher or more porous. That builds in a little margin and matches how most ceilings are actually painted, with a well-loaded roller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage also differs between products. Paints with more solids often hide better per coat even if they spread over slightly less area, which can mean fewer coats overall. Our <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">ceiling paint picks</a> compare hide across the main options, which matters as much as the spread rate when estimating.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Label Ranges (Interior Latex)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">350-400</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft/gal, smooth primed</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Lower</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Textured or porous</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1 qt</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Spot priming a room</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">+10%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Waste margin</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="texture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Texture and Porosity Change the Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Texture increases the real surface area and the amount of paint needed to fill it. Light textures such as orange peel need a little more paint than smooth drywall. Knockdown and heavier textures need noticeably more. Popcorn ceilings are the extreme case: the irregular surface has far more area than the flat ceiling measurement suggests, and the texture soaks up paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough planning adjustment, reduce your coverage figure by about 10 to 15 percent for light texture, 20 to 30 percent for heavy texture and up to 40 to 50 percent for popcorn. These are rules of thumb, not manufacturer figures, and actual use varies widely. For popcorn in particular, buy extra and be ready to return unopened cans.
          </p>

          <p>
            Porosity matters as much as texture. New drywall, fresh joint compound and bare plaster absorb paint heavily. That is why they need a primer coat first, which is far cheaper than using extra topcoat to seal them. Over a properly primed surface, the topcoat will cover at close to its label rate. Our <a href="/best-primer/">primer guide</a> covers drywall and stain-blocking primers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Old flat paint that has chalked, or a ceiling that has not been painted in many years, can also be more absorbent than expected. If the first coat seems to disappear into the surface, that is a sign of porosity, and it is worth adjusting your estimate for the second coat. Our guide to <a href="/ceiling-paint-roller-marks/">ceiling roller marks</a> explains how porous patches also cause flashing.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Planning coverage adjustment</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smooth, previously painted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Use label (low end)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light orange peel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reduce about 10-15%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Knockdown or heavy texture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reduce about 20-30%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Popcorn</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reduce about 40-50%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">New drywall (after primer)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Use label; prime first</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="coats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Coats to Plan For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plan for two coats on most ceilings. A single coat can be enough when you are refreshing a clean ceiling in the same white with a good paint, but it often leaves thin spots or roller marks that only show in daylight. Budgeting for two coats is the safer choice, and leftover paint is useful for touch-ups later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Color changes need two coats at minimum. Going from a yellowed old white to a fresh bright white, or from a tinted ceiling to white, almost always takes two. Going from a dark color to white may need a primer coat plus two finish coats. Stains need a stain-blocking primer before the finish coats, regardless of color.
          </p>

          <p>
            New drywall needs a full coat of drywall primer and then two coats of paint. Skipping the primer on new drywall does not save paint; the first topcoat acts as an expensive primer and you end up needing a third. Prime first, then calculate two topcoats at the label rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are painting the ceiling the same color as the walls, which many people do with a flat or matte wall paint, plan the ceiling and walls together. Our comparison of <a href="/ceiling-paint-vs-wall-paint/">ceiling paint and wall paint</a> covers when that approach makes sense and which paint to use.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Primer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Topcoats</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Refresh, same white, good condition</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spot prime patches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Yellowed white to fresh white</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spot prime</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark or tinted to white</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full primer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stains (water, smoke)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stain-blocking primer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">New drywall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drywall primer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="examples" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Worked Examples
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small bathroom, 5 by 8 feet, smooth ceiling in good condition, two coats. Area is 40 square feet; two coats is 80 square feet. At 350 square feet per gallon, that is about 0.23 gallons. A quart covers roughly 87 square feet at that rate, so one quart is just enough, but buying a gallon gives you room for mistakes and future touch-ups and often costs little more than two quarts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bedroom, 12 by 14 feet, smooth ceiling, two coats. Area is 168 square feet; two coats is 336 square feet. At 350 square feet per gallon, that is about 0.96 gallons. One gallon is technically enough but leaves no margin; adding 10 percent brings the need to about 1.06 gallons, so one gallon plus a quart, or two gallons if you want leftovers or plan to paint a closet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Living room, 16 by 20 feet, knockdown texture, two coats. Area is 320 square feet; two coats is 640 square feet. With a 25 percent texture adjustment, coverage drops to about 263 square feet per gallon, giving roughly 2.4 gallons. With a 10 percent margin, buy three gallons. If the room has a large closet or alcove, add it before rounding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Open-plan living and kitchen, 24 by 30 feet, popcorn ceiling, two coats. Area is 720 square feet; two coats is 1,440 square feet. With a 45 percent popcorn adjustment, coverage drops to about 193 square feet per gallon, giving roughly 7.5 gallons. A five-gallon bucket plus three single gallons is a sensible purchase, with any unopened gallons returned. Spraying popcorn often uses more paint again, so check with your sprayer's guidance.
          </p>

