        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Buying, Boxing and Leftovers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy all the paint for a ceiling at once, from the same batch if possible. Tinted paints in particular can vary slightly between batches, and even whites can differ a little. If you are buying several gallons, mix them together in a five-gallon bucket before you start, which is called boxing. That ensures the whole ceiling is one consistent color and sheen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Five-gallon buckets are usually cheaper per gallon than single gallons and are convenient for larger ceilings, because you can roll straight from the bucket with a grid. For a single small room, single gallons are fine. Many stores accept returns of unopened, untinted cans, which makes it safer to buy a little extra.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep leftover paint for touch-ups. Label the can with the room and date, seal it tightly by tapping the lid down with a mallet over a rag, and store it somewhere that does not freeze. Ceilings need touch-ups less often than walls, but water stains and fixture changes happen, and having the original paint makes repairs blend.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not forget the other supplies that scale with ceiling size. Larger ceilings need more roller covers, especially if the texture is rough, plus primer for patches, painter's tape if the walls are finished and enough drop cloths to cover the floor. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-ceiling/">how to paint a ceiling</a> lists the full kit.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy for two coats and round up.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Running out partway through a ceiling forces a stop that leaves a lap mark. It is far better to have a quart or a gallon left over, labelled for touch-ups, than to have to stop and buy more mid-job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Top of the Label's Coverage Range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage ranges assume ideal conditions. Ceilings are usually rolled heavily, and many are textured or slightly porous. Use the low end of the range for smooth ceilings and reduce it further for texture, or you will run short partway through.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting Closets, Alcoves and Slopes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A closet or alcove can add a meaningful share to the ceiling area, and vaulted ceilings have much more area than the floor below. Measure every part you are painting, and use the slope length rather than the floor for vaulted ceilings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Primer to Save Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On new drywall or porous patches, the first topcoat acts as a primer and soaks in, so you need an extra coat anyway. A coat of primer costs less than an extra coat of paint and gives a more even finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating Popcorn Ceilings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Popcorn texture has far more surface than its flat footprint and absorbs a lot of paint. Plan for coverage to drop sharply, buy extra and keep receipts so you can return unopened cans.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Boxing Multiple Gallons</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small differences between cans can show on a ceiling under raking light. Mix all the gallons for one ceiling together in a five-gallon bucket before you start, so the color and sheen are consistent everywhere.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much paint do I need for a 12x12 ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12 by 12 foot ceiling is 144 square feet. Two coats is 288 square feet. At around 350 square feet per gallon on a smooth ceiling, that is about 0.82 gallons, so one gallon is enough with a small margin. Add more for texture, porous surfaces or a closet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a gallon of ceiling paint cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most interior latex paints, including ceiling paints, list coverage of roughly 350 to 400 square feet per gallon on a smooth, primed surface. Check the specific product's label. Use the low end of the range, and reduce it further for textured or porous ceilings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do ceilings need two coats of paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do. One coat can be enough to refresh a clean ceiling in the same white, but two coats give more even color and hide roller marks better. Color changes, new drywall and stained ceilings need primer and two coats. Budget for two coats.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much more paint does a popcorn ceiling need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A lot more. As a rule of thumb, plan for coverage to drop by roughly 40 to 50 percent compared with a smooth ceiling, because the texture has much more surface and absorbs paint. Actual use varies, so buy extra and return unopened cans.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a quart enough to paint a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for very small ceilings. A quart covers roughly 85 to 100 square feet per coat on a smooth surface. That is enough for two coats on a small bathroom or closet ceiling of about 40 square feet. For anything larger, buy a gallon.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a five-gallon bucket for a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For large ceilings or several rooms, yes. Five-gallon buckets are usually cheaper per gallon, let you roll straight from the bucket with a grid and ensure consistent color. For a single small room, one or two single gallons are enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate paint for a vaulted ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure the slope length from the wall to the peak, multiply by the room length and double it for a simple gable ceiling. If you cannot measure the slope, adding roughly 20 to 40 percent to the floor area gives a rough estimate, but measuring is more reliable.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

